💡 **SUGGESTION: dead code — the `!i.status` branch in `normalizeOpenStatuses` is unreachable**

`js/common/findingRegistry.js:718-723`:

```js
function normalizeOpenStatuses(items) {
    items.forEach(function(i) {
        if (!isOpenStatus(i.status)) return;
        if (!i.status) i.status = STATUS_OPEN;   // ← never runs
    });
}
```

`isOpenStatus(i.status)` is true only for the truthy strings `'open'`/`'re-introduced'`, so by the time the second guard is evaluated `i.status` is always truthy — `if (!i.status)` can never fire and the helper is a no-op under its current name.

Two readings:

1. **The intent was to delete the helper** (payload items always carry a status from `createIdentity`) — then remove the function and its call in `registryFromPayload`; the current fail-closed outcome (a malformed status-less item stays out of the dedup space) is the correct new-by-default behavior and is already covered by tests.
2. **The intent was to default malformed items to `open`** — then the guards are in the wrong order (`if (!i.status)` must be checked *before* `isOpenStatus`). Note that reading widens the dedup space for corrupt payload data, which cuts against invariant 1's bias — option 1 (delete) is the better fit.

No behavior change either way today; flagged only so the next reader doesn't assume status normalization actually happens here.

💡 **SUGGESTION: stage-1 auto-merge inspects only the top-ranked item — a same-file near-identical match can be shadowed out of both merge and confirmation**

`js/common/findingRegistry.js:386`:

```js
var best = ranked[0] || null;
if (isAutoMerge(best, file, severity)) { ... }
var proposed = topProposals(ranked, policy);
```

`rankedOpenMatches` sorts purely by similarity (ties by item n). If a **cross-file or cross-class** open item scores even slightly higher than a **same-file near-identical** one, `ranked[0]` is the cross-file item, `isAutoMerge` rejects it (correctly — invariant 1 scope), and the same-file ≥ 0.92 match is never auto-merged. With the default `candidatesPerFinding: 1`, `topProposals` then offers only the cross-file item for confirmation, so the same-file match isn't even asked about — it lands as a plain `new` identity.

Consequences are invariant-safe: this produces a harmless *split* (an extra thread / unmerged identity), never a masked merge — exactly the cheap error direction the design prefers, which is why this is a SUGGESTION and not higher. It also needs a contrived registry (two open items with near-identical claims where the foreign one outscores the local one) to trigger at all.

If you want stage 1 to keep its near-identical auto-merge in the shadowed ordering, consider letting the predicate scan the ranked list instead of only its head, e.g. pass `ranked` and look for the first entry satisfying the `isAutoMerge` triple (same file + same class + ≥ `AUTO_MERGE_SIMILARITY`); `topProposals` could similarly ensure the same-file ≥ 0.92 entry is always among the offered candidates. Current behavior is acceptable as-is.

